<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brigham Young</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1801">1801</a>–<a href="/years/1877">1877</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Brigham Young was the second president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-jesus-christ-of-latter-day-saints">Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints</a>, leading it from 1844. He organised the migration of the Mormons from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salt-lake-valley">Salt Lake Valley</a> in 1847 and directed the settlement of much of the Great Basin. He headed the proposed State of Deseret and was the first governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah-territory">Utah Territory</a> from 1850 to 1858.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brigham-young.jpg" alt="Brigham Young" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles William Carter (1832 - 1918) Details on Google Art Project · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_William_Carter_-_Brigham_Young_-_Google_Art_Project_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Brigham Young was born on 1 June 1801 in Whitingham, Vermont, the ninth child of John Young, a farmer, and Abigail Howe. The family moved to Chenango County in upstate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> when he was three. He had little formal education, though his mother taught him to read and write. In 1830 his brother Phineas gave him a copy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-mormon">Book of Mormon</a>, and in 1831 missionaries of the Latter Day Saint movement came to preach in Mendon, where he was drawn by their practice of spiritual gifts such as speaking in tongues. On 14 February 1835 he was ordained a member of the Quorum of the Twelve Apostles.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Young moved to Missouri in 1838, the year Governor Lilburn Boggs signed the Mormon Extermination Order, and organised the migration of the Latter Day Saints to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a>. In 1844, while he was travelling to gain support for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-smith">Joseph Smith</a>'s presidential campaign, Smith was killed by a mob, which began a succession crisis. After negotiating a ceasefire, Young was unanimously elected the church's second president in 1847. He led his followers west from Nauvoo and arrived in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salt-lake-valley">Salt Lake Valley</a> on 24 July 1847, a date now observed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah">Utah</a> as Pioneer Day.</p><p>He founded Salt Lake City, and President Millard Fillmore appointed him the first governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah-territory">Utah Territory</a> and superintendent of Indian affairs. He directed settlements across a wide area of the West, issued a selective extermination order against male Timpanogos, and later made peace with Native Americans. As governor he allowed polygamy and supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and its expansion into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah">Utah</a>, and he formalised the prohibition of black men from the priesthood.</p><p>After President James Buchanan appointed a new governor, Young declared martial law and reactivated the Nauvoo Legion, which began the Utah War. During it the Utah Territorial Militia carried out attacks that killed at least 120 members of the Baker-Fancher wagon train, the Mountain Meadows Massacre. The war ended in 1858 when Young surrendered, agreeing to resign as governor and let federal troops enter the territory in return for a pardon.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Young died in Salt Lake City on 29 August 1877, probably of peritonitis from a ruptured appendix. He had at least fifty-six wives and fifty-seven children. His teachings fill the nineteen volumes of the Journal of Discourses, and Rodman W. Paul credited him with directing the founding of 350 towns between 1847 and 1877.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Brigham Young” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After a long trek from Illinois, <b>Brigham Young</b>'s vanguard company entered the Great Salt Lake Valley; he reportedly declared 'This is the right place'.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-24">24 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Brigham Young</b> later led most of his followers west to Utah.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-27">27 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Brigham Young</b> was elected governor, and church officers filled the legislature and courts, so civil and religious authority were effectively one.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-deseret">State of Deseret</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Whitingham, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in Salt Lake City, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.78,-72.87&amp;year=1850" aria-label="Whitingham on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2788 644 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Brigham Young"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3238 914h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3238 914h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.78,-72.87&amp;year=1850">What was here in 1850? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
